{Three passed pawns in the centre and on the both flanks look very dangerous. Can a sickly black knight do anything against such power?} 1. d7 Na7 2. d8=Q $1 (2. h6 $143 Nc6+ 3. Kb5 (3. Kb6 $144 Kd6 $19) Kd6 4. d8=Q+ Nxd8 5. a7 Nc6 $1 6. a8=Q Rb4+ 7. Ka6 Kc7 $1 $19) Nc6+ 3. Kb5 Nxd8 4. a7 Rb4+ $1 (Kxf5 $144 5. a8=Q Ne6 6. Qg8 Nc7+ 7. Kc5 Ne8 8. h6 Nxf6 9. h7 $18) 5. Kxb4 Nc6+ 6. Kc4 $3 ({Logical (thematic) try} 6. Kc5 $143 Nxa7 7. h6 Kxf6 {White in zz 1. The zugzwang is quite unexpected. Not only due to material advantage that promises to last long as the knight has enough freedom to escape his aggressor. The main surprise comes from a pair of pawns: g5 and f2. They are placed at the distance of one move! A spare tempo that usually helps winning battles for zugzwangs has no effect here because the pawns don't oppose each other. The white pawn is in duel against theblackknight(correspond ing squares f2-a7, f3-c8)! And the black pawn fights vs. king (corresponding squares g5-c5, g4-d7) ! Another unusual thing here - squares c5 and d7 are neighboring! The white king jumps from one corresponding square to another like a knight!} 8. Kb6 (8. f3 $143 Nc8 $19 {zz 2.}) Nc8+ 9. Kc7 Na7 $1 10. Kd7 g4 $1 {(zz 3.)} 11. Ke8 Nc6 12. Kf8 Ne5 13. h7 Kxf5 14. Kg7 (14. h8=Q $143 Ng6+ $19 {Third fork!}) Ng6 $11) Nxa7 7. h6 Kxf6 8. Kc5 {Black in zz 1!} g4 ({main line II} Nc8 $144 9. f3 $1 {(zz 2.)} Na7 10. Kb6 Nc8+ 11. Kc7 Ne7 (Na7 $144 12. Kd7 $18 {bonus zz, not seen in the logical try.}) 12. h7 Nd5+ (Kg7 $144 13. f6+ $1 $18) 13. Kd6 $1 (13. Kc6 $143 Nb4+ 14. Kb5 Kg7 $11) Kg7 14. Kxd5 Kxh7 15. Kd6 $1 $18 (15. Ke5 $143 Kg7 $11)) 9. Kb6 Nc8+ 10. Kc7 {with: main line A} Na7 ({main line B} Ne7 $144 11. h7 Nd5+ 12. Kd6 $1 Kg7 13. Kxd5 Kxh7 {Similar pawn ending, but a pair of pawns is one rank higher. The white king corrects his move.} 14. Ke5 $1 (14. Kd6 $143 Kh6 $11) Kg7 15. Kf4 $18 {win.}) 11. Kd7 {(zz 3.)} Nb5 12. Ke8 Nd4 13. Kf8 Kxf5 14. h7 Ne6+ 15. Kg8 $18 {(e8) win. ---------------- Multivariate study of contemporary style.} 1-0

