{If the knight didn't interfere with his queen Pg2 would have been pinned and White could mate in a couple of moves. Consequently, the knight is allowed to do whatever he wants to. The capture of black Pd6 is a nice tactical move, but it is only a logical (thematic) try.} 1. Na5 $3 {Quiet transfer of the knight to e3.} ({Logical (thematic) try} 1. Nxd6 $143 Kh2 2. Qf3 Nf2 3. Qg3+ Kh1 4. Qf3 Kh2 5. Nc4 g1=Q 6. Nxe3 d6 $1 {White in zz!} 7. Nd1 $1 Qxd1 8. Qxf2+ Kh1 9. Kh3 Qb3+ 10. e3 Qe6+ 11. Kg3 Qe5+ $1 $11 {draw.}) Kh2 ({Black can try to get rid of his pawn} d5 $144 2. Qxd5 Kh2 3. Qxd1 {but his knight falls too} (3. Qf3 $143 Nf2 4. Nc4 g1=Q 5. Nxe3 d6 $11 {zz}) g1=Q 4. Qxg1+ Kxg1 5. Kg3 $1 $18) 2. Qf3 Nf2 3. Nc4 $1 (3. Qf4+ $143 Kh1 4. Qf3 Kh2 5. Nc4 $18 {loss of time}) g1=Q 4. Nxe3 {Black in zz! His spared Pd6 blocks the square. King, queen and knight are totally paralyzed. And what is wrong withthepawnsmoves?} e5 (d5 $144 5. Nd1 $1 Qg2 6. Qxf2 d4 7. Qf4+ Kh1 8. Nf2+ Kg1 9. Nh3+ Kh1 10. Qxd4 $18) 5. Nd1 $1 Qxd1 ({The knight cannot desert his post.} Nxd1 $144 6. Qh3#) 6. Qxf2+ Kh1 7. Kh3 (7. Kg3 $143 Qg1+ $19) Qb3+ 8. e3 Qe6+ 9. Kg3 $1 $18 {win. Black cannot play Qe5+. An evil chance seldom comes alone - because of the blocking on d6 another pawn has blocked square e5. This combination of material and tempo logical ideas with a deep logical try was called as "Krikheli forsight effect" by Andrey Visokosov. "I dedicate my study to him for his development of this interesting and complex theme in his creative work."(S.Didukh)} 1-0

